pivot()

pivot() changes a DataFrame from long format to wide format by using one column as rows, another as columns, and another as values.

===

Syntax:

df.pivot(index="row_column", columns="column_column", values="value_column")
Real-time example

Suppose we have sales data:

import pandas as pd

data = {
    "Name": ["John", "John", "Mary", "Mary"],
    "Product": ["Laptop", "Mobile", "Laptop", "Mobile"],
    "Sales": [50000, 20000, 60000, 25000]
}

df = pd.DataFrame(data)

print(df)

----------

Original data:

| Name | Product |  Sales |
| ---- | ------- | -----: |
| John | Laptop  | 50,000 |
| John | Mobile  | 20,000 |
| Mary | Laptop  | 60,000 |
| Mary | Mobile  | 25,000 |


====

Now:

result = df.pivot(
    index="Name",
    columns="Product",
    values="Sales"
)

print(result)

Result:

| Name | Laptop | Mobile |
| ---- | -----: | -----: |
| John | 50,000 | 20,000 |
| Mary | 60,000 | 25,000 |


====

What happened?
Name     → index (rows)
Product  → columns
Sales    → values

So pivot() basically rearranges the data.

